Veranda Solenoid
Ingredients: ELECTRO SWING + DREAM POP & SHOEGAZE + TROPICAL & SUNNY + Cello + 2010s ERA
Creative Catalyst: It was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule.

A hallucinatory collision of architectural swing and molten optics, this genre sounds like a brass band melting slowly inside a greenhouse at noon. The heavy, syncopated 1920s Charleston beats have been violently dragged through a wall of 2010s sidechained reverb, then smeared with the sun-bleached, tape-warped gloss of tropical lounge music. The signature of the sound is the bizarre tension between rigid, algorithmic electro-swing percussion and the wide, horizon-swallowing wash of shoegaze fuzz guitars, all anchored by a weeping, low-end cello section that plays harsh, aggressive jazz pizzicato through distorted tube amps. 

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 118 BPM, marching with an intoxicated, limping swing that hiccups lazily against a suffocating wall of shimmering pedal-board haze.
- Harmonic Palette: Major-seventh chords drowning in flanged tape degradation, oscillating between breezy Hawaiian slack-key major scales and minor-key Parisian cafe despair.
- Production Signatures: Over-compressed acoustic drum loops sidechained to an infinite plate reverb; electric cello bowed so hard it clips the digital preamps into warm sand; vinyl crackle layered beneath high-gloss tropical marimba samples.
- Dynamics: The music breathes like a fever dream—drifting in long, stagnant pools of sunlit ambient haze before suddenly exploding into frantic, glitching electro-swing brass riots, only to collapse back into a humid hush.

